The purpose of this Code is to establish a uniform administrative framework for the governance, management, and operation of Blevins Holdings LLC (“Blevins Holdings” or the “Company”), including its departments, controlled subsidiaries, programs, personnel, facilities, systems, funds, records, and administrative activities.
This Code is intended to:
1. Establish clear lines of authority and accountability;
2. Provide uniform administrative standards;
3. Define the responsibilities of departments and officials;
4. Establish procedures for delegation, approval, documentation, and oversight;
5. Promote effective internal controls and responsible stewardship of Company resources;
6. Provide a framework for enterprise-wide compliance, risk management, and internal oversight; and
7. Provide an orderly system for issuing and maintaining administrative rules.

Unless otherwise provided, this Code applies to:
1. Members of the Board when acting in an administrative or delegated capacity;
2. Officers;
3. Employees;
4. Departments and offices;
5. Controlled subsidiaries to the extent directed by the Company or applicable subsidiary governance documents;
6. Contractors, consultants, agents, and other third parties where required by contract or written Company directive;
7. Company-owned, leased, managed, or controlled facilities;
8. Company information systems, records, funds, property, and programs; and
9. All administrative activities undertaken on behalf of Blevins Holdings.

This Code is an internal administrative instrument. Except where expressly required by law or contract, it does not:
1. Create an employment contract;
2. Alter the at-will status of any employee;
3. Create a private cause of action;
4. Confer enforceable rights upon third parties; or
5. Expand the authority of any official beyond authority otherwise lawfully granted.
Chapter 2. Administration of the Code

The Chief Executive Officer shall designate a department or official to serve as Code Administrator. The Code Administrator shall:
1. Maintain the official administrative codification;
2. Assign chapter, article, and section numbers;
3. Maintain amendment and revision records;
4. Coordinate publication of approved amendments;
5. Maintain superseded provisions as required by the records retention schedule;
6. Identify conflicting or obsolete administrative provisions; and
7. Periodically recommend technical or substantive revisions.

The Code Administrator may make non-substantive technical corrections without separate substantive approval, including:
1. Correcting typographical errors;
2. Renumbering provisions;
3. Updating cross-references;
4. Correcting department or position names following approved reorganizations; and
5. Standardizing formatting.
Technical corrections may not alter substantive duties, authority, rights, controls, or approval requirements.
T I T L E 2 Organization and Administration
Chapter 3. Enterprise Organization

Each division shall be administered by a division head designated pursuant to applicable organizational authority. A division head shall:
1. Direct the operations of the department;
2. Implement applicable Board Policies, executive directives, and provisions of this Code;
3. Establish appropriate internal procedures and controls;
4. Maintain records of material decisions and approvals;
5. Administer personnel and resources assigned to the department;
6. Coordinate with other departments where responsibilities overlap;
7. Escalate material legal, financial, security, operational, or compliance matters;
8. Maintain appropriate segregation of duties; and
9. Perform other duties lawfully assigned.

Authority assigned to an official may be delegated when:
1. Delegation is not prohibited by law or governing documents;
2. The delegating official possesses the authority being delegated;
3. The delegation is consistent with applicable approval matrices and internal controls; and
4. The delegation is documented when required by this Article.

A delegation shall be in writing when it concerns:
1. Contract execution;
2. Expenditure or payment approval;
3. Personnel actions;
4. Regulatory certification;
5. Access to restricted information or systems;
6. Disposition of material Company assets;
7. Settlement of claims or disputes;
8. Execution of legal documents;
9. Authority ordinarily reserved to an officer or department head; or
10. Any matter designated by policy as requiring written delegation.

A written delegation should identify:
1. The delegating official;
2. The delegate;
3. The authority delegated;
4. Any monetary, subject-matter, geographic, or
5. The effective date;
6. The expiration date, if applicable;
7. Whether subdelegation is permitted; and
8. Any reporting or documentation requirements.
organizational limits;

Company administrative issuances may include:
1. Board Policies;
2. Provisions of this Administrative Code;
3. Executive directives;
4. Enterprise standards;
5. Departmental policies;
7. Manuals;
8. Control standards;
9. Technical standards;
10. Guidance documents; and
11. Temporary administrative instructions.
6. Standard operating procedures;

Subordinate administrative issuances shall be consistent with superior authority. Unless expressly provided otherwise, the order of precedence is:
1. Controlling law;
2. Governing documents;
3. Board action and Board Policy;
4. This Administrative Code;
6. Enterprise policies and standards;
7. Departmental policies;
8. Procedures and manuals; and
9. Guidance and informal instructions.
5. Executive directives;

Each controlled administrative issuance shall have an identified owner responsible for:
1. Maintaining its accuracy;
2. Reviewing it periodically;
3. Coordinating required approvals;
5. Maintaining related procedures; and
6. Initiating retirement or replacement when obsolete.
4. Communicating material changes;

Exceptions to mandatory administrative requirements may be granted only by an authority empowered to approve the exception. Material exceptions shall be documented and include:
1. The requirement affected;
2. The reason for the exception;
3. The approving authority;
4. Compensating controls, if applicable;
5. The duration of the exception; and
6. Required review or expiration date.
T I T L E 3 Financial and Commercial Administration
Chapter 6. Financial Administration

The Company shall maintain books and financial records that accurately and reasonably reflect transactions, assets, liabilities, revenues, expenses, and other material financial activity. No person shall knowingly:
1. Create or approve a false or misleading financial entry;
2. Maintain an undisclosed or unrecorded Company fund or asset;
3. Circumvent required approval controls;
4. Alter supporting documentation for an improper purpose; or
5. Direct another person to mischaracterize a transaction.

Financial processes shall be designed, to the extent practicable, to separate:
1. Transaction initiation;
2. Approval;
3. Custody of assets;
4. Payment execution;
5. Accounting or recording; and
6. Reconciliation or review.
Where full segregation is impracticable, compensating controls shall be documented.

Company procurement shall, as appropriate to the transaction:
1. Serve a legitimate business purpose;
2. Be supported by sufficient budget authority;
3. Promote reasonable competition;
4. Consider cost, quality, risk, performance, security, and reliability;
5. Avoid prohibited conflicts of interest;
6. Be appropriately documented;
7. Receive required approvals before commitment; and
8. Be conducted in accordance with applicable sanctions, vendor-risk, and compliance requirements.

Competitive quotations or proposals shall be obtained when required by procurement policy or when prudent based on transaction value, risk, complexity, or market conditions.
The Company may use sole-source procurement when justified by circumstances including:
1. Unique technical requirements;
2. Compatibility;
3. Intellectual property restrictions;
5. Continuity requirements;
6. Absence of reasonable alternatives; or
7. Other documented business justification.
4. Emergency conditions;

Risk-based due diligence shall be performed before engaging vendors that may:
1. Access sensitive Company information;
2. Access Company systems;
3. Handle material Company funds;
4. Interact with regulators or government officials on behalf of the Company;
5. Perform regulated activities;
6. Create material operational dependency; or
7. Present significant legal, financial, reputational, security, or compliance risk.
T I T L E 4 People, Information, and Operations
Chapter 8. Personnel Administration

Hiring, promotion, transfer, compensation changes, disciplinary actions, leave administration, separation, and other material personnel actions shall be:
1. Authorized by an appropriate official;
2. Documented as required;
3. Processed through approved systems;
4. Consistent with applicable law and Company policy; and
5. Subject to required Human Resources review.

Access to Company systems shall:
1. Be based on legitimate business need;
2. Be approved by authorized personnel;
3. Observe least-privilege principles where practicable;
4. Be reviewed periodically;
5. Be modified promptly following role changes; and
6. Be revoked promptly following separation or loss of business need.

Use of artificial intelligence, machine learning, automated decision systems, and generative technologies for Company business shall comply with approved standards governing:
1. Data confidentiality;
2. Information security;
3. Privacy;
4. Intellectual property;
5. Human review;
6. Accuracy and validation;
7. Records retention;
8. Regulatory compliance;
9. Vendor risk; and
10. Prohibited or restricted use cases.
Chapter 11. Facilities and Administrative Operations

Company property shall be:
1. Used primarily for authorized Company purposes;
2. Protected against loss, theft, misuse, and unauthorized disposition;
3. Inventoried where appropriate;
4. Returned upon request or separation; and
5. Disposed of through authorized processes.

The General Counsel shall serve as the principal legal advisor to the Company within the scope of the General Counsel’s engagement and authority. The General Counsel may:
1. Advise the Board, officers, departments, and subsidiaries on legal matters;
2. Coordinate outside counsel;
3. Establish legal review requirements;
4. Direct or coordinate litigation and material disputes;
5. Issue or approve legal holds;
6. Advise concerning attorney-client privilege and work-product protection;
7. Review material contracts and transactions as required;
8. Advise concerning corporate governance; and
9. Perform other legal functions assigned by competent authority.

The Company’s compliance program may include:
1. Policies and standards;
2. Training;
3. Risk assessments;
4. Monitoring;
5. Testing;
7. Investigations;
8. Corrective actions;
9. Third-party controls; and
10. Reporting to executive management and the Board.
6. Reporting mechanisms;

Confirmed deficiencies may result in corrective measures including:
1. Process changes;
2. Additional controls;
3. Training;
4. Restitution or financial recovery;
5. Vendor remediation;
6. Personnel action;
8. Referral to executive management;
9. Referral to the Board or an appropriate Board committee;
10. Referral to law enforcement or regulators when authorized or required; or
11. Other measures appropriate to the circumstances.
7. Disciplinary action;
Chapter 14. Office of Inspector General

Subject to its charter, Board Policy, and applicable law, OIG may:
1. Conduct audits, reviews, inspections, investigations, and evaluations;
2. Review allegations of fraud, waste, abuse, misconduct, or significant control failure;
3. Evaluate internal controls and administrative processes;
4. Issue findings and recommendations;
5. Monitor corrective actions;
6. Refer matters to Legal, Human Resources, Compliance, executive management, or the Board;
7. Recommend policy or control improvements; and
8. Perform other oversight functions assigned by competent authority.

For administrative purposes, the Company’s subsidiary portfolio may include:
1. Blevins Energy Systems (BES);
2. Blevins Data Infrastructure (BINFRA);
3. Blevins Defense (BDEF);
4. Blevins Real Estate (BRES);
5. Blevins Health (BHEALTH);
6. Blevins Pharma (BRX);
7. Blevins Research and Development (BRD);
8. BCOMPANY; and
9. Any other entity subsequently established, acquired, or designated as a subsidiary.

The Board or other competent authority may establish matters requiring approval by Blevins Holdings before action by a subsidiary, including:
1. Annual budgets;
2. Material capital expenditures;
3. Material indebtedness;
4. Issuance of equity or ownership interests;
5. Material acquisitions or dispositions;
6. Formation or dissolution of entities;
8. Entry into new regulated business lines;
9. Material related-party transactions;
10. Appointment or removal of specified officers;
11. Material contracts above established thresholds; and
12. Other matters designated as reserved.
7. Material litigation settlements;

Enterprise policies may be made applicable to subsidiaries by:
1. Board action;
2. Subsidiary governing documents;
3. Written shareholder or member action;
4. Intercompany agreement;
5. Executive directive issued under appropriate authority; or
6. Formal adoption by the subsidiary’s governing body.
Application of an enterprise policy shall account for subsidiary-specific legal, regulatory, licensing, contractual, and operational requirements.

Subsidiaries shall provide reports reasonably required for enterprise governance, including information concerning:
1. Financial performance;
2. Compliance;
3. Material litigation;
4. Regulatory matters;
5. Cybersecurity;
6. Significant operational risks;
7. Material contracts;
8. Capital expenditures;
9. Internal-control deficiencies; and
10. Other matters identified by Blevins Holdings.
Chapter 16. Intercompany Administration

Blevins Holdings may provide centralized services to subsidiaries, including:
1. Finance and accounting;
2. Human resources;
3. Legal services;
4. Compliance;
5. Information technology;
6. Cybersecurity;
7. Procurement;
8. Communications;
9. Facilities;
10. Internal audit and oversight;
11. Risk management; and
12. Administrative support.

Managers and supervisors are responsible for taking reasonable measures to:
1. Communicate applicable requirements;
2. Maintain appropriate controls;
3. Address known deficiencies;
4. Escalate material concerns; and
5. Prevent or correct repeated noncompliance.

Suspected material violations should be reported through an appropriate channel, which may include:
1. A supervisor or department head;
2. Global Human Resources;
3. Legal, Risk, and Compliance;
4. The General Counsel;
6. An authorized ethics or reporting channel;
7. Executive management; or
8. The Board or appropriate Board committee when warranted.
5. OIG;

No waiver or exception may be granted if doing so would violate:
1. Applicable law;
2. Governing documents;
3. A binding regulatory obligation;
4. A contractual restriction;
5. A Board-reserved requirement; or
6. A requirement expressly designated as non-waivable.

An authorized official may approve a temporary exception when:
1. A legitimate business justification exists;
2. Risks have been assessed;
3. Appropriate compensating controls are implemented where practicable;
4. The exception is documented;
5. The exception has a defined duration; and
6. Required legal, compliance, security, financial, or other review has occurred.

Nothing in this Code prevents an authorized official from taking reasonable emergency action necessary to protect:
1. Human life or safety;
2. Company facilities or property;
3. Information systems or data;
5. Legal rights;
6. Regulatory standing; or
7. Other substantial Company interests.
4. Business continuity;
Emergency action outside ordinary procedures shall be documented and reported to the appropriate authority as soon as practicable.
T I T L E 9 Final Provisions
Chapter 20. Effective Date and Transition

The Code Administrator, in consultation with appropriate departments, shall coordinate periodic review of this Code and recommend amendments necessary to address:
1. Changes in law or regulation;
2. Changes in Company organization;
3. Audit or investigation findings;
5. Identified control deficiencies;
6. Changes in enterprise risk; and
7. Obsolete or conflicting provisions.
4. Material changes in operations;
